why 4 1/4 LEAPS After its diminished leap down to D the alto turns back up by a fourth to G, inside the leap, and then steps down F–E♭. It returns inward, by a fourth instead of a step.
why 4 1/2 DIRECT The tune falls E♭–C while the bass leaps G–C into the cadence: hidden octaves, common at a cadence where the melody is given.
why 5 1/2 CROSSING The alto drops a fifth to C while the tenor rises to E♭, so they trade places for a beat. The alto climbs back D–E♭ over the next eighths.
why 5 1/2 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Soprano and alto move from a unison G to an octave C in contrary motion. Bach accepts octaves by contrary motion; strict exercises avoid them.
why 5 5/8 CROSSING The alto climbs back to D, still under the tenor's E♭; on the next beat it is above again.
why 7 1/2 DOUBLING The tune leaps up to C while the tenor steps B♭–C; both land on the third of the A♭ chord.
why 8 1/4 LEAPS After the tune's leap down it keeps falling F–E♭ into the fermata. Bach keeps the hymn melody.
why 8 1/2 RANGE At the fermata the bass drops to a deep E♭ for a full, resonant cadence.
why 10 1/4 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass move from E♭ to F in octaves, with the bass's C in between. The bass skips through the chord, which for Bach is enough to separate them; strict exercises still count it.
why 10 1/4 SPACING The bass falls to a low F, two octaves under the tenor, for weight before the dominant. It climbs back an octave on the next eighth.
why 10 1/4 LEAPS The bass falls E♭–C–F, outlining the C minor chord before landing on the root of ii°. Then it leaps back up an octave.
why 12 1/4 SIX_FOUR The ⁶₄ comes on beat 2 and its V on beat 3, before the fermata: the cadence is placed in the middle of the bar, where the phrase ends.
why 13 1/4 DIRECT The tune leaps down C–G while the bass leaps E♭–C, both within the C minor chord. A hidden fifth inside one harmony.
why 16 0 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass go from A♭ to G in unison, with the bass dipping to F in between. For Bach the bass's skip separates them; strict exercises still count it.
